@meta
id: 13-15
title: Either, or
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-groups, regex-verymagic
par: 68
minutes: 6
vscode: two searches, and remember which lines you already tagged
open: worker.log

@brief
Two failure reasons in this worker log are the infrastructure team's
problem rather than ours: `SFTP_UNREACHABLE` and `SCHEMA_INVALID`.
Every line carrying either of them gets ` escalated=true` on the end.
There are four such lines. The other errors — declines, missing orders,
a bad signature — are ours and stay untagged.

One pass, one pattern. A pattern can offer alternatives.

@keys
| `\|` | alternation in the default dialect: this or that |
| `\v` then `\|` | under very magic the bar is bare: `\vthis\|that` |
| `A` then `n.` | append, next match, repeat |

@hints
Do not do two searches. A single pattern can name both reasons and
match whichever one it finds first.
--
The bar means "or". In very magic it is just `|`; in the default
dialect it is `\|`. Either side can be as long as you like.
--
`/\vSFTP_UNREACHABLE|SCHEMA_INVALID` `Enter`, then `A escalated=true`,
`Esc`, then `n.` three times.

@solution
/\vSFTP_UNREACHABLE|SCHEMA_INVALID<Enter>  A escalated=true<Esc>  n.  n.  n.  :wq<Enter>

Sixty-two keystrokes, and most of them are the two reason codes, which you
would have had to type anyway.

Alternation is the lowest-precedence thing in a pattern. The bar splits
the *entire* pattern into branches, so `payout\.export|settle` is not
"payout dot, then export or settle" — it is `payout\.export` or, quite
separately, `settle`, anywhere it appears. If you want the alternation
to cover only part of the pattern you need to fence it in, and that is
what the next lesson's brackets are for.

Branches are tried left to right at each position, and the first one
that matches wins, so `/\vord|order` will stop at the `ord` inside
`order`. Put the longer alternative first, or add `>` on the end.

The bar is one of the characters where the two dialects disagree most
visibly. `\|` is how vim's own help writes it and how you will see it
in every vimrc; under `\v` it is the bare `|` that the rest of the
world uses. Same operator, two spellings, and knowing that is most of
what "magic levels" comes to.

